WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:04.097
This is a hydraulic press at a parts manufacturer.

2
00:00:04.818 --> 00:00:08.823
Today it gets a die change: the tool that shapes the parts comes out.

3
00:00:09.874 --> 00:00:11.952
First look at where the energy is.

4
00:00:12.685 --> 00:00:16.168
Electricity from the cabinet, for the motor and the controls.

5
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.660
Compressed air, for the clamps.

6
00:00:19.365 --> 00:00:22.732
Oil under pressure in the accumulator, next to the pump.

7
00:00:23.476 --> 00:00:26.715
And the heavy ram: it is raised, so it can fall.

8
00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:32.417
Two of them stay even when everything is off: the oil pressure and the raised ram.

9
00:00:33.188 --> 00:00:34.546
That is stored energy.

10
00:00:35.215 --> 00:00:39.767
You have to release it or block it, or something can still move after switching off.

11
00:00:40.814 --> 00:00:43.844
And the emergency stop, the red button on yellow?

12
00:00:44.551 --> 00:00:47.280
It stops the press, but it isolates nothing.

13
00:00:48.016 --> 00:00:52.800
Someone can reset it, the controls can fail, and the stored energy stays.

14
00:00:53.638 --> 00:00:57.225
An emergency stop is a safety function, not a lockout.

15
00:00:58.651 --> 00:01:01.600
That is why you work with LOTO, lockout-tagout.

16
00:01:02.432 --> 00:01:06.287
You shut off every source of energy, and fit your own padlock to it,

17
00:01:06.414 --> 00:01:07.749
with a tag with your name.

18
00:01:08.754 --> 00:01:11.204
Start with the LOTO procedure for this press:

19
00:01:11.332 --> 00:01:15.546
often one page with a photo of every point where you shut off a source,

20
00:01:15.732 --> 00:01:16.719
in the right order.

21
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:21.591
Then the technician tells the operators what he is going to do,

22
00:01:21.766 --> 00:01:24.808
so nobody switches the press back on in surprise.

23
00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:29.316
Next he stops the press the normal way, with the stop button,

24
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.081
and parks the ram at the top.

25
00:01:32.452 --> 00:01:33.416
Now isolate.

26
00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:36.773
First the main switch on the cabinet: off.

27
00:01:37.468 --> 00:01:40.475
The pump motor and the controls get no more power.

28
00:01:41.562 --> 00:01:43.652
Then the compressed-air valve: closed.

29
00:01:44.389 --> 00:01:48.220
A lockable valve with an exhaust lets the air behind the valve escape,

30
00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:50.891
so the line to the press loses its pressure.

31
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.764
The switch and the valve are the isolation points:

32
00:01:54.938 --> 00:01:58.131
the places where you shut off a source and can fit a lock.

33
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.078
On both he fits his own lock, with a tag with his name.

34
00:02:03.068 --> 00:02:06.957
The lock really keeps the switch off, the tag tells who is working.

35
00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:09.806
A tag without a lock does not count.

36
00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.814
Are more people working on the press, as in a big die change?

37
00:02:15.608 --> 00:02:19.520
Then the keys of the isolation locks go into a group lockbox.

38
00:02:20.534 --> 00:02:23.773
Every worker puts his own personal lock on that box.

39
00:02:24.491 --> 00:02:26.848
Not just the team leader for the whole crew:

40
00:02:27.034 --> 00:02:29.472
everyone who reaches in has his own lock.

41
00:02:30.504 --> 00:02:34.602
So the keys only come free when the last worker has taken his lock off.

42
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:39.518
As long as one person is still working, the press cannot be switched on.

43
00:02:40.871 --> 00:02:42.287
Now the stored energy.

44
00:02:42.976 --> 00:02:46.412
Step one: a mechanical safety block under the raised ram.

45
00:02:47.113 --> 00:02:50.922
If it comes down, it lands on the block, and not on a hand.

46
00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:56.081
Step two, and only after that: bleed the hydraulic accumulator.

47
00:02:56.789 --> 00:02:59.297
That is a tank that stores oil under pressure.

48
00:03:00.035 --> 00:03:03.135
It stays under pressure, even when the pump has stopped.

49
00:03:04.197 --> 00:03:07.041
He opens the bleed valve and watches the gauge.

50
00:03:07.748 --> 00:03:10.639
The needle falls, until it reads 0 bar.

51
00:03:11.678 --> 00:03:12.468
Why this order?

52
00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:17.732
Bleed the pressure first, and the ram can come down before the block is under it.

53
00:03:18.481 --> 00:03:23.032
Oil pressure alone does not hold a load up reliably: a valve can leak.

54
00:03:24.351 --> 00:03:27.056
Everything is isolated, locked and released.

55
00:03:27.788 --> 00:03:29.228
Still he does not assume it.

56
00:03:29.953 --> 00:03:32.345
First he closes the guard: with it open,

57
00:03:32.624 --> 00:03:35.863
the interlock often stops the press even without a lock,

58
00:03:36.072 --> 00:03:37.651
and the test proves nothing.

59
00:03:38.457 --> 00:03:40.280
Then he presses the start button.

60
00:03:41.028 --> 00:03:42.108
Nothing happens.

61
00:03:43.137 --> 00:03:47.212
That start attempt, with the gauge at zero and the block under the ram,

62
00:03:47.352 --> 00:03:49.500
proves the press is at zero energy.

63
00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:54.029
For electrical work you also measure that no voltage is left.

64
00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.402
Then he sets the controls back to off,

65
00:03:57.565 --> 00:04:00.201
so nothing happens when the energy comes back.

66
00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:03.777
Only then does he open the guard and reach in.

67
00:04:04.766 --> 00:04:07.355
Does something move during the test after all?

68
00:04:08.132 --> 00:04:11.198
Then a source was missed, such as a second air tank,

69
00:04:11.383 --> 00:04:13.508
or the wrong switch was isolated.

70
00:04:14.252 --> 00:04:16.272
Better now than with your hand inside.

71
00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.748
The new die is in.

72
00:04:19.437 --> 00:04:24.209
Before restarting: tools out, the safety block too, then the guards closed,

73
00:04:24.395 --> 00:04:26.206
and everyone clear of the press.

74
00:04:26.919 --> 00:04:28.661
If the ram rests on the block,

75
00:04:28.869 --> 00:04:32.642
the press's LOTO procedure says how the block comes out safely.

76
00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:34.529
Then the locks.

77
00:04:35.272 --> 00:04:37.884
Only the person who fitted a lock removes it.

78
00:04:38.617 --> 00:04:40.823
The technician removes his own locks and

79
00:04:40.858 --> 00:04:43.285
tells the operators the press can run again.

80
00:04:44.287 --> 00:04:46.516
Has someone gone home with his lock still on?

81
00:04:47.248 --> 00:04:50.871
Then that lock comes off only under a strict written procedure:

82
00:04:50.998 --> 00:04:52.566
try to reach that person,

83
00:04:52.705 --> 00:04:55.862
check the machine is safe, and let a manager decide.

84
00:04:56.606 --> 00:04:57.918
Never just cut it off.

85
00:04:58.941 --> 00:05:02.018
If you work on the electrical installation itself,

86
00:05:02.157 --> 00:05:05.663
the five safety rules for electrical work apply as well.

87
00:05:06.375 --> 00:05:10.288
And ask on every floor: can I see the LOTO procedure for this machine?
